{"author":{"id":"6c7374a500bfd4cb191ae45b3f9d4b7a3d9d7f0324d04add217e8cbefeb7e1b9"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1792367021,"nonce":"Q9MK+w2b5JSi7O3z08Yi7jScCF0=","message":"**Done in 2e42d89, with two differences from what was asked**\n\nCodeEditor takes `minimap` (off by default; CodeView takes it too) and `minimapWidth` (the widest it gets, 96; a quarter of the editor's width when that is less). The strip is one canvas drawn once per revision, a moment (0.12 s) after the last change, so typing is not slowed; scrolling moves only the slider. Each line is a row 2 px high, each run of tokens of one colour is a rectangle, whitespace is skipped, folded lines are left out (it draws `document.visible`), and the colours are read from the editor's own token styles, so a theme or `--ui-code-*` change reaches it. Pressing the strip scrolls the editor to that line, dragging the slider scrolls as it moves from where it was taken, and the wheel over the strip scrolls the editor. Styled by `--ui-code-minimap-bg`, `--ui-code-minimap-border`, `--ui-code-minimap-slider`, `-hover` and `-active`. CodeComponents has the checks, and tools/snapshot/scenes/CodeEditorMinimap.hx renders it in both schemes.\n\nNot as asked:\n- A document with more lines than the strip has rows is drawn smaller to fit and shows every few lines, so the strip is always the whole document and never scrolls proportionally. A canvas that moves rebuilds its mesh (the triangles are made in screen units), so scrolling the strip would rebuild every row on each scroll step; scrolling would need CanvasPainter to translate a built mesh.\n- Blocks only, no legible characters.\n\nStill to come, as the issue said: markers for the selection, search matches and diagnostics, with 495aa31's decorations. Left open for RayDE to try it.","files":null}]}